INT. STARLITE LANES - SHOE COUNTER - 3 A.M.

The bowling alley is empty and dim, only the pin machines still thumping in the back lanes. Rows of rental shoes in cubbies. A cash register with a taped-up drawer. On the wall, a faded poster of a dancing pin.

LEANDRA CHO, 17, in a red vest too big for her, sits on a stool behind the counter, doing homework she is not doing. Across from her, MR. DOBRYNIN, 60s, the night manager, in a cardigan, polishes a bowling shoe with a rag, slowly, as if there were nothing else in the world.

MR. DOBRYNIN
You're early tonight.

LEANDRA
I'm always early.

MR. DOBRYNIN
Not by two hours.

LEANDRA
Couldn't sleep.

MR. DOBRYNIN
Mm.

He holds the shoe to the light, finds a speck, rubs it. She turns a page of her textbook. Neither looks at the other.

LEANDRA
Mr. Dobrynin, can I ask you something?

MR. DOBRYNIN
You can ask.

LEANDRA
Did you always work here?

MR. DOBRYNIN
Thirty-one years.

LEANDRA
Before that?

MR. DOBRYNIN
Before that I was a bus driver.

LEANDRA
Why'd you quit?

MR. DOBRYNIN
I didn't like the people.

LEANDRA
And you like these people?

MR. DOBRYNIN
These people are going somewhere very slowly. A bus is going somewhere on a schedule. I prefer slowly.

LEANDRA
That's kind of sad.

MR. DOBRYNIN
Is it?

LEANDRA
They just roll a ball and it hits things.

MR. DOBRYNIN
And then they roll another one.

LEANDRA
And it hits less.

MR. DOBRYNIN
And then they go home.

She smiles. It is a small, private smile.

LEANDRA
My dad used to bring me here.

MR. DOBRYNIN
Did he?

LEANDRA
Sundays. He'd get me the cheese fries and the shoes in size one.

MR. DOBRYNIN
I remember.

LEANDRA
You don't.

MR. DOBRYNIN
Gray carpet jacket. Smoked a pipe outside.

LEANDRA
(quietly)
He did.

MR. DOBRYNIN
Gave me a dollar every time. Said it was for the shoes.

LEANDRA
It was always for the shoes.

MR. DOBRYNIN
He was a generous man.

LEANDRA
He was a man who left.

MR. DOBRYNIN
Both things.

She picks at the corner of her textbook. He sets down the shoe, takes another.

LEANDRA
What do you say to someone who's mad at someone who's gone?

MR. DOBRYNIN
You don't say anything. You hand them a pair of shoes.

LEANDRA
That's not an answer.

MR. DOBRYNIN
It's the only one I have.

LEANDRA
Size what?

MR. DOBRYNIN
You tell me.

LEANDRA
Seven.

MR. DOBRYNIN
Lane twelve is open.

LEANDRA
It's three in the morning.

MR. DOBRYNIN
The pins don't know.

She looks at him. He looks at the shoe. After a moment she slides off the stool and walks to the cubbies, finds a seven, and holds it up.

LEANDRA
These?

MR. DOBRYNIN
Those are fine.

LEANDRA
Will you come?

MR. DOBRYNIN
I have to polish.

LEANDRA
Mr. Dobrynin.

MR. DOBRYNIN
(a long breath)
One frame.

He sets down the rag. She is already pulling on the shoes.

LEANDRA
You'll hate how bad I am.

MR. DOBRYNIN
I've seen worse.

LEANDRA
Who?

MR. DOBRYNIN
Your father.

She laughs, a real laugh this time, wet at the edges, and grabs the stool, steadying herself. Mr. Dobrynin comes around the counter in his socks, shoes in one hand, and the two of them walk together down the dark carpet toward Lane Twelve.
